## 6. Extremely Simple Continuous Deployment

Deployment must be triggered automatically after merging into the protected main branch.

The preferred flow:

```text
Pull Request
     |
     v
GitHub Actions CI
     |
     v
Required Checks Pass
     |
     v
Merge to main
     |
     v
Build Immutable Artifact
     |
     v
Deploy to Staging
     |
     v
Smoke E2E Tests
     |
     v
Deploy to Production
     |
     v
Health Verification
```

Where the hosting platform supports it, promote the same tested artifact instead of rebuilding.

### Deployment requirements

- One immutable release artifact.
- One documented deployment mechanism.
- No manual SSH commands for normal deployments.
- No manually copied files.
- No manually edited production configuration.
- No secrets committed to Git.
- No unnecessary deployment orchestration.
- No custom deployment framework.
- No mandatory human intervention for routine releases.

Use GitHub Actions environments and scoped permissions.

Prefer OIDC-based authentication when supported by the deployment provider.

Pin third-party GitHub Actions to immutable commit SHAs.

Use concurrency controls to prevent conflicting deployments.

### Deployment strategy

Prefer the simplest strategy supported by the hosting environment that provides safe recovery.

Possible approaches:

- Atomic artifact replacement.
- Container replacement.
- Rolling deployment.
- Blue/green deployment when justified.

Do not introduce Kubernetes solely to obtain deployment automation.

### Database migrations

Migrations must be:

- Version-controlled.
- Deterministic.
- Automatically applied through a controlled deployment step.
- Compatible with the deployment strategy.
- Tested against representative database state.

Prefer backward-compatible expand/contract migrations.

Do not assume arbitrary destructive migrations can be rolled back safely.

### Rollback

Provide a documented and tested rollback procedure.

Application rollback should redeploy the previous immutable artifact.

Database recovery must be handled separately and safely.

Do not automatically reverse destructive migrations.

